members.description:
Define a page. Declare what it takes and returns at the top, then its
behaviour in one of two forms. `setup(page)` is the form for lifecycle
hooks and per-instance state: it runs once per instance, before `onLoad`,
and returns its hooks and `actions`. `actions: page => ({ … })` is the
short form for a page with only actions; it also runs once per instance.

```ts
export default definePage({
  params: type<{ id: string }>(),
  data: { order: null as Order | null },
  setup(page) {
    return {
      async onLoad({ id }) { const order = await load(id); page.update(d => { d.order = order; }); },
      actions: { close() { return page.close(); } },
    };
  },
});

export default definePage({
  data: { count: 0 },
  actions: page => ({ bump() { page.update(d => { d.count += 1; }); } }),
});
```

Whether an open, navigation, or update was refused because the host is
older than the lxapp's `minRuntime`.

The rejection's `code` is `E_NOT_SUPPORTED`, which also covers missing
capabilities; only this says the user needs a newer host app.

```ts
try {
  await lx.nav.go({ app: appId })
} catch (error) {
  if (hostUpgradeRequired(error)) showUpdateHostNotice()
  else throw error
}
```

The language in effect right now, as a canonical BCP-47 tag. Map it to
the catalogs this lxapp actually ships and fall back where it has none;
that narrowing is yours, and is not a language setting of its own.

examples:
const task = lx.media.compressVideo(clip, { quality: 'medium' });
for await (const event of task.progress) render(event.progress);
const { path, size } = await task.result;

members.description:
The handle of an lxapp live as a surface of its own, declared or not;
`undefined` when it is not. A live lxapp has one handle: `lx.surface.openApp`,
`getApp` and `getDeclared` of the declaration that shows it return the same one.

examples:
lx.ui.toast('Saved', { tone: 'success' });
const busy = lx.ui.toast('Uploading…', { loading: true, durationMs: 60_000 });
busy.dismiss();

members.description:
Read why an open was refused, or `null` when it failed for another reason.

Navigation rejects like any other `lx` API; the reason rides on `data.code`,
the same way a surface error carries its own. Branch on it rather than on the
message: `maintain` deserves "try again later" and `suspended` does not.

```ts
try {
  await lx.nav.go({ app: appId })
} catch (error) {
  const reason = lxAppUnavailableReason(error)
  if (reason === 'maintain') showMaintenanceNotice()
  else if (reason === 'suspended') showUnavailableNotice()
  else throw error
}
```

The page that opened this one, by `lx.nav.go` or `lx.surface.openPage`;
absent for a root, a tab, after a restart, and while `setup` runs (read
it in a hook or an action).

types.description:
What `lx.shell.setAppDisplay` changes. Each key it carries is set;
`undefined`, `null` or `''` restores the default, and a key left out
keeps its value.

Result of `lx.clipboard.readText`. Branch on `status`.
A copied empty string is `{ status: 'ok', text: '' }`;
an image-only clipboard is `{ status: 'empty' }`.

